# Bitvectors, and solving bitvector equations through cvc5.
#
# See demos/README.md for how to run this script.

# Alongside the Boolean algebras, Tau supports the theory of bitvectors. An
# external SMT solver, cvc5, is used to decide bitvector constraints; the core
# language and its algorithms do not depend on it.

# A bitvector type carries its width, so bv[8] is the type of 8-bit vectors and
# bv[16] of 16-bit ones. Widths are not fixed by the implementation: pick the one
# the problem needs. Constants are written like any other Boolean algebra
# constant, in braces with the type appended:

n {30}:bv[8]

# Constants may also be given in hexadecimal, with a #x prefix:

n {#x1f}:bv[8]

# The arithmetic operators are modular, wrapping at the width of the type. Note
# that + is bitvector addition and has nothing to do with the Boolean operators
# of demo_1.1 -- there, exclusive or is spelled ^:

n {10}:bv[8] + {20}:bv[8]
n {200}:bv[8] + {100}:bv[8]   # wraps: 300 mod 256
n {50}:bv[8] - {20}:bv[8]
n {10}:bv[8] * {20}:bv[8]
n {100}:bv[8] / {10}:bv[8]

# Bit shifts and the bitwise negated connectives !& !| !^ (nand, nor, xnor) are
# available too, and a term can be cast to a bitvector type with (bv[n]):

n {1}:bv[8] << {3}:bv[8]

# The precedence of the bitvector operators is unusual, and worth knowing before
# writing anything dense: / binds tighter than *, and - binds tighter than +.
# The full order, loosest last, is
#
#   (bv[n]) > ' > & > ^ > | > !& > !^ > !| > / > * > % > - > + > << > >> > fex > fall
#
# Every operator sits on its own level, so parenthesize whenever the reading is
# not obvious.

# Equations over bitvectors are solved with the same solve command used in
# demo_2.1. A linear equation, where the answer wraps around the width:

solve x:bv[16] + y:bv[16] = {1}:bv[16]

# Nonlinear equations are fine as well, since cvc5 decides them:

solve x:bv[16] * y:bv[16] = {512}:bv[16] && x:bv[16] = {8}:bv[16]

# Remainders, and a constraint with a unique answer:

solve x:bv[8] % {2}:bv[8] = {1}:bv[8]

# sat, unsat and valid work over bitvectors too. Modular arithmetic has zero
# divisors, so a nonzero 8-bit value can square to zero -- 16 * 16 = 256, which
# wraps to 0:

sat x:bv[8] * x:bv[8] = {0}:bv[8] && x:bv[8] != {0}:bv[8]

# Note that lgrs is a Boolean-equation procedure and does not apply here; see
# the end of demo_2.1-solver.tau.

quit
